WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.912 --> 00:00:03.072
- [Narrator] This is no 4th of July.

00:00:03.072 --> 00:00:05.600
This is the national pyrotechnic festival

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.267
in Tultepec, Mexico.

00:00:08.221 --> 00:00:10.804
(upbeat music)

00:00:16.707 --> 00:00:20.165
Each year, the city throws
the Pamplonada Pirotécnia

00:00:20.165 --> 00:00:21.875
or National Pyrotechnic Festival

00:00:21.875 --> 00:00:24.325
for nine days in March
to celebrate the feast

00:00:24.325 --> 00:00:28.492
of John of God, the patron
saint of firework-makers.

00:00:30.056 --> 00:00:33.085
Local families, businesses,
and firework factories

00:00:33.085 --> 00:00:34.995
build enormous bull-shaped floats

00:00:34.995 --> 00:00:37.595
out of paper mache called torritos,

00:00:37.595 --> 00:00:40.136
the construction of which can take months.

00:00:40.136 --> 00:00:41.240
The exterior of these floats

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.568
are completely covered with
decorations and fireworks.

00:00:44.568 --> 00:00:45.571
This is all in preparation

00:00:45.571 --> 00:00:47.635
for the festival's most popular event,

00:00:47.635 --> 00:00:49.835
the burning of the bulls.

00:00:49.835 --> 00:00:51.507
The bulls are paraded through the town

00:00:51.507 --> 00:00:53.736
and brought into the
square in front of crowds

00:00:53.736 --> 00:00:56.673
chanting (fuego) or fire.

00:00:56.673 --> 00:00:58.430
A courageous soul is then sent in

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.347
to light up each float.

00:01:04.416 --> 00:01:06.816
As the bulls are pulled
through the fearless crowd

00:01:06.816 --> 00:01:08.605
the fireworks illuminate the town

00:01:08.605 --> 00:01:12.027
and the party rages on into the night.

00:01:12.027 --> 00:01:14.610
(upbeat music)

00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:22.150
(electronic music)

